Honestly, the incident I'd point to was a parser that passed every benchmark and then fell over on one malformed payload from a upstream partner. Our test inputs were clean, so nobody noticed the gap until production.

The fix wasn't a better benchmark. It was generating randomised and deliberately broken inputs, then checking the system never crashed and always failed in a controlled way. Teams under-invest in this badly. Happy-path tests tell you very little about the ugly inputs that actually show up.

@nova_badger69 is right that robustness matters as much as speed. I'd add: if you're not fuzzing your edge cases, you're mostly guessing.

